---
title: "A custom view, wired by hand"
description: "The same stepper, captured client-side in JavaScript this time, with an author-written picture standing in for the automatic bars."
---

Every other page in this exhibit takes the picture Taliesin draws automatically. This one
takes it over. Two cells cooperate:

- the traced cell below is a `{js}` generator, not `{python}` this time; each `yield` is
  one recorded step, captured entirely in the browser with no kernel involved,
- a second, ordinary `{js}` cell reads the stepper's current step with `tali.frame()` and
  draws its own picture instead of asking for the automatic bars.

{{< input name="n" type="slider" min="5" max="8" step="1" value="6" label="array size" >}}

::: {.debug name="sort"}
```{js}
//| trace: true
//| input: n

function shuffle(count) {
  const a = Array.from({ length: count }, (_, i) => i + 1);
  for (let i = a.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
    const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
    [a[i], a[j]] = [a[j], a[i]];
  }
  return a;
}

function* bubble(a) {
  for (let i = 0; i < a.length; i++)
    for (let j = 0; j < a.length - i - 1; j++) {
      yield { a, i, j };
      if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) [a[j], a[j + 1]] = [a[j + 1], a[j]];
    }
}

return bubble(shuffle(tali.value("n")));
```
:::

```{js}
//| input: sort
const f = tali.frame("sort");
const written = new Set((f.changed.a && f.changed.a.writes) || []);
const row = document.createElement("div");
row.style.cssText = "display:flex;gap:.4rem;align-items:center;font-family:monospace;";
(f.locals.a || []).forEach((v, i) => {
  const chip = document.createElement("span");
  chip.textContent = String(v);
  chip.style.cssText =
    "min-width:1.8rem;text-align:center;padding:.35rem .2rem;border-radius:999px;" +
    (written.has(i) ? "background:#f97316;color:#fff;" : "background:#334155;color:#e2e8f0;");
  row.appendChild(chip);
});
return row;
```

Move the slider and the trace re-captures at the new size: `//| input: n` on the traced
cell is what tells the stepper to re-run itself when the input changes, the same
mechanism any other reactive `{js}` cell uses.
